<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Basilica of San Isidoro</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">11th–12th centuries</span> · Also known as Royal Collegiate Church of San Isidoro</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Basilica of San Isidoro is a Romanesque church in León, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, built to house the relics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isidore-of-seville">Isidore of Seville</a>, brought there in 1063, and consecrated in its rebuilt form in 1149. Its Pantheon of the Kings, the burial place of the Leonese royal family, preserves 12th-century wall paintings, including a calendar of the labours of the months, among the best-preserved Romanesque murals in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/basilica-of-san-isidoro.jpg" alt="Basilica of San Isidoro" width="320" height="215" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: José Luis Filpo Cabana · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bas%C3%ADlica_de_San_Isidoro_de_Le%C3%B3n._Fachada_principal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
